docs/research/sources/P4-welfare-decision-brief.md:75:The standards recognize this fragility but differ in specificity. UEP is the clearest on handling mechanics: birds in cage-free systems must be caught and held **upright with both hands** or carried by **both legs with no more than 3 birds in each hand**; they must not be carried by a single leg, wing, head, neck, or tail; feed withdrawal for end-of-lay hens must be no more than **18 hours before the beginning of catching** if they will be transported, or **24 hours** if culled on-farm; and water must **not** be withdrawn prior to catching. Certified Humane requires a depopulation action plan and keeps the total time from start of loading to completion of unloading of transport trays to **less than 10 hours**, with water available until catching begins. RSPCA requires approved catching teams, a depopulation action plan, vehicles parked as near as possible to the house, and, in hot weather above **21°C**, transport at night or in the coolest part of the day or a **20% reduction in stocking density**. citeturn37view0turn37view4turn37view3turn44view3turn44view13turn44view7

